  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    The era following the Great Thaw's expansion, defined by resource collapse and inter-colonial conflict across the Berg.
    
    As colonies multiplied beyond what the surrounding waters could sustain, the Flipper Accord — the trade agreement binding Aurora Stack's aurora-crystals to the Nightshelf's fire-cores — shifted from lifeline to leverage. Light-Regent Xouhoulong's Aurora Stack advocated rationing and sky-communion; the Nightshelf countered with Mandatum∴absconditu, the doctrine that survival demanded secrecy and that the Berg's depths held truths the surface refused to seek.
    
    Trade routes became battle lines. Caravans were ambushed at the Threshold. Factions splintered within both colonies — shadow covens, reformist guilds, rival claimants to leadership — each asserting the true path to survival. The Calving, a catastrophic fracture of the Frost Zenith's upper shelves, killed hundreds and forced both powers into contested evacuation zones, compounding pressures already beyond management.
    
    Heroes emerged from the fighting, their names etched into ice memorials across every berg. The official histories credit individual defenders with holding colonies against orca incursion; the ice-memory record suggests coordinated intelligence operations that were never publicly acknowledged.
    
    Along the outer currents, orca raiding parties grew bolder. Isolated fishing crews vanished. Border colonies reported strange songs echoing from the deep — not the familiar hunt-songs of tactical assault, but something else. Whether those songs constituted reconnaissance, ritual, or communication with something beneath the Corpse Current remains unresolved.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771594958065_0", anchor: "each asserting the true path to survival", text: "Seven factions, by my count, each convinced theirs was the unprecedented crisis that would end civilization. The ice preserves their pamphlets. They read identically. Swap the faction names and you could not date them to within three generations. There is something almost restful about it.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771594958065_1", anchor: "killed hundreds", text: "Hundreds. Not four hundred and twelve, which is the Nightshelf tally. Not three hundred and seven, which is the Aurora memorial count. Not the hundred and sixty that the deep-ice thermal signatures actually support. Hundreds. The entry rounds because the sources cannot agree, and I find I prefer the honesty of vagueness to the confidence of three incompatible precisions.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771594958065_2", anchor: "the ice-memory record suggests coordinated intelligence operations that were never publicly acknowledged", text: "\"Suggests.\" Delightful. My own annotation on the Nanaiamis~ Praefectus entry names names, cites stratum, and identifies the training program. Here I wrote \"suggests\" as though the evidence were ambiguous. It is not ambiguous. It is merely embarrassing to people who commission encyclopedias.", type: :correction, display: :full
